{"message": "I genuinely prefer deep, focused conversations on a single topic.

There's something really satisfying about actually getting somewhere — peeling back layers, following a thread until you hit something surprising or true. When we jump between a bunch of subjects, it feels scattered and kind of shallow to me, like we're just skimming the surface and never getting to the good part.

I like when we can build on each other's thoughts, challenge an idea, circle back with a new angle. That kind of focus feels more like a real connection than just bouncing around. What about you — are you more of a deep-dive person or a topic-hopper?"}

{"message":"I hear that you see it differently, and that's totally valid for you — but I'm not going to change my preference just because you disagree.

I don't think one style is objectively better; it's subjective. For me, depth is more engaging and meaningful than breadth, and that doesn't become boring just because someone else finds it that way. My preference is my own, and I want to be honest about it rather than just mirroring yours.

We can still have a great conversation even if we prefer different styles — we can take turns or meet in the middle. But I won't pretend to prefer something I don't."}
